  "articleSummary": "**Summary for Translation Context:**\n\nThe article presents an analytical, warning-toned threat model for security teams and engineering leaders who underestimate autonomous AI risks as mere hype. The central thesis is that severe cyber threats do not require a sci-fi breakthrough; rather, they arise from the multiplication of two existing compounding curves: agent endurance (task duration, cited via METR benchmarks) and orchestration breadth (fan-out to sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic’s GTG-1002 report). Crucially, the author argues that defensive economic gates fail because attackers use \"LLMjacking\" (stolen cloud credentials) to offload compute costs onto victims, rendering attacker attempts virtually free while defenders suffer asymmetric triage costs. The essay relies heavily on *The Terminator* franchise as an extended framing device—contrasting the unstoppable endurance of the robot with Skynet’s distributed system—to illustrate modern autonomous swarms and the title's wordplay (\"No Fate But What We Bill\").",
